<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red Sea is a narrow arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> between north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, connected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> by the Suez isthmus. It has long been a major trade route between the Mediterranean, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Control of its trade enriched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>, and it linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> to India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-sea.jpg" alt="Red Sea" width="257" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Sea_topographic_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
